Date: Thu, 4 Mar 93 14:15:11 CST
From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: MISC: Weapons in public places

>But is exposing people to risk an act of force?  My impression is that
>it is LC to carry weapons of any caliber; it is only at the point
>where the slug enters the body of a non-consenting person that force
>has been applied and "the law" has been violated.
>Dale Worley

This is not my understanding.  I take it that libertarians prohibit both
assault and battery.  Assault is an act intended to put a reasonable person
in imminent fear of an offensive touching, which does indeed induce such a
fear.  A libertarian would thus not demand that you wait until a bullet
enter your body to defend yourself; you could pop off the first shot if it
were the only reasonable way to save yourself.

How does this apply to bearing weapons?  So long as one had a peaceable
intent, it would not preclude possessing even highly destructive weapons. 
But one who evinced the intent to initiate coercion could face justified
preemptive strikes.  
Note that it is much easier for a reasonable person to feel threatened by
weapons of massive destruction.

But this is just a theoretical take on the subject.  I suspect that even a
libertarian society would enforce mutually-agreed upon regulations on
nuclear and biological weapons.  One practical rule might be this: citizens
of a private community can possess any weapon except one that would
necessarily create a disproportionate response to an invasion of person or
property.  

I won't pretend this dissipates all the grey areas, but it is a start.


