From: twb3@midway.uchicago.edu (Tom Morrow)
Subject: POLI: BATF (was: My Home Town)

Scott DeLancey makes some trenchant comments about the BATF:

>     But it wasn't the local authorities that were involved.  It was
>the Bureau of *Alcohol*, *Tobacco*, and *Firearms*.  This agency has
>no jurisdiction in matters of kidnapping, rape, child abuse, murder,
>or any such crimes that may have been committed in the Davidians 
>compound.  (In fact one can argue, from a point of view that is far short
>of anarchism or radical libertarianism, that the BATF, like the DEA, is
>a law enforcement agency that has *no* real crimes in its purview, and
>thus no legitimate claim to any support or sympathy from honest citizens).

The history of the BATF bears this out.

The BATF traces its roots back to Alexander Hamilton, who in 1791 created
an agency to enforce the much hated national tax on whiskey that led to the
Whiskey Rebellion.  This rebellion, which took most prominent form in the
Pennsylvania backcountry, provides ample evidence that many Americans did
*not* consent to the new national government formed by the Constitution. 
G. Washington crushed the rebellion with an overpowering use of force.

The BATF later rose to power by hunting down those who violated the
Prohibition's regime.  Still later, it took on the enforcement of federal
firearm regulations and tabacco taxes.

Today the BATF escapes the sort of scrutiny normally applied to federal law
enforcement agencies because, thanks to Hamiliton's scheme, it falls under
the jurisdicition of the Treasury Dept.  The FBI, by contrast, is
controlled by the Justice Dept.  

Interesting, isn't it, that this agency seems to have thrived on punishing
people for using recreational chemicals?  


