Subject: META/D-FENS: Defending Ourselves, and Flames

I hate to argue the basics, but when the list is inflamed with gun control
arguments, with calls for nonviolence and pacifism, with claims that we are
supporting Christianity over Paganism, and with calls for action by the
White House, how can we sit back and say nothing?

For example:

Andy Wilson writes:

>My own take on self-defense is that the defender has the right to prevent
>the violation of their rights by using the minimal amount of force
>necessary to get the job done.  This would never result in intentional
>killing of the attacker,  although it could result in unintentional
>killing.  There are plenty of ways to incapacitate an attacker short
>of killing them.  I don't believe killing is ever necessary.

Andy is entitled to his opinion, of course, but I must challenge it.

The problem with "minimal amount of force" is that it often gets you
killed. By the time you've escalated your level of force to exactly match
that of the attacker, he (or she) has time to escalate to a much higher
level, perhaps lethally.

The rule of thumb is that you _never_ aim a gun at someone unless you plan
to shoot them (cops are exempted from this, for various reasons, such as
"covering" a perp).

And you _never_ shoot them unless you are shooting to *kill*. You go for
chest shots, sometimes head shots. You don't try to "wing" them--that's a
Hollywood myth. 

If I'm attacked, I know that dialing "911" will do me little good. If the
response comes at all, it'll likely be too late. And if the attackers are
Feds, it'll do no good at all.

What can a person do in an era of "home invasions" (gangs breaking into
homes, sometimes executing all those they find, etc.) and "no knock"
searches by the corrupt Federales? Respond with a "minimal amount of
force"? Practice nonviolence? Reason with them? Ask for divine guidance?

Me, I keep a semiautomatic Heckler and Koch MP5 (SP-89 version) loaded with
60 rounds of Winchester Silvertip hollowpoints at the ready at all times.
When the "Bureau of Alcohol, Tobacco, Firearms, and Computers" arrives, I
expect to take at least several of them with me. And I'll be justified. (I
hope it doesn't come to this, but if it does, at least I'll be ready.)

Killing those who would try to limit my freedom or tax me seems fair to me.
The Feds are goons. 

For those who want to compromise with the Feds, I hope you like the smell
of Zyklon B, as that's what you'll eventually get from them. The oft-quoted
words of Rev. Niemoller (sp?) are still true, the bit about "...and when
they came for me, there was no one left to protest."

The deaths of the BATF agents, and their murder of a 2-year old baby (and
probably others) may send a message out that people need to arm themselves
against this kind of "no knock" action. Remember the similar cases all over
the country--the black man shot in his own yard in Houston for having the
gall to investigate the noises made by SWAT members crawling through his
yard, the San Diego man killed in his bed as he reached for a handgun
during a "wrong address" no knock search, the Randy Weaver case in Idaho,
and the many raids on Black Panthers a generation ago.

Predictably, the media and politicians are calling for new laws to get
"dangerous weapons" out of the hands of the public. Buy guns now! (Karate
and Kung Fu may be nice, but guns are more reliable.)

(By the way, if you follow the Waco discussions closely you'll see some
strong evidence of Fed incompetence and police state behavior: the Feds
could've seized David Koresh when he was out jogging (but didn't), the Feds
approached the house surreptitiously (making no attempts to first give
Koresh and the "cult" followers a chance to come out), and the Feds fired
_first_ (though that wasn't needed for the cult to begin shooting). The
evidence that the Feds fired first is in the videotapes, no longer being
shown, which showed a handgun carred by a Fed going off, perhaps
accidentally--a look of surprise on his face and then both sides opened
fire.)

In recent days, some have posted denunciations of my views,  calling them
"offensive" and "immature." I've gotten private e-mail along these lines,
as well. Some of these comments border on being flames or flame bait,
especially the "cascades" adding their denunciation to the earlier ones, 
but I will not lodge any kind of formal complaint....I can fight fire with
fire, when the appropriate time comes. 

(As a complete aside, one of the current problems with PPL justice on this
list is the bizarre and unpredictable way it's enforced and the punishments
meted out. If I complain--not that I plan to--the likely verdict is _one_
warning for the  flame baiter and _one_ warning for _me_, perhaps for
failing to use "META:" in the right places, or for "coloring outside the
lines." (a joke)  This is not so much a complaint about how the list is run
