Your challenge is most welcome.

   The problem with "minimal amount of force" is that it often gets you
   killed. By the time you've escalated your level of force to exactly match
   that of the attacker, he (or she) has time to escalate to a much higher
   level, perhaps lethally.

I clearly need to elaborate on what I mean by "minimal use of force".

When dealing with non-deadly force,  it is possible to defend yourself
without harming your oppenent,  if you are skilled enough.  I was able
to successfully resist a mugging with defensive techniques that I learned
studying T'ai Chi Chuan,  a "soft" form of Kung Fu.  However, I don't
think in general it is reasonable to expect people to always be able
to discern whether they are faced with non-deadly force or deadly force
in a self-defense situation.

I absolutely agree that when you make a defensive action against deadly 
force,  you must be able to be as certain as possible that you are going
to remove the threat (unless you are hopelessly outnumbered).

There exist weapons that can instantly incapacitate without killing,
involving the use of debilitating chemicals or electric shock.  It's true
that at this point a gun is still the most reliable form of self-defense,
but enough money invested in non-deadly self-defense technology would 
solve this problem.

In the case where you only have deadly force available to meet deadly
force,  your right to use it is clear.  But conceivably in some society
it could be seen as the responsibility of the defender to make their
best effort to have non-deadly force available to them.

